WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.758
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.058 --> 00:00:08.665
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.965 --> 00:00:16.853
Unit ten: The Attention Economy. In this unit, you will analyse how technology competes for our attention.

4
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.269
attention economy.

5
00:00:19.969 --> 00:00:25.231
attention economy means a system where companies compete to capture and sell our attention.

6
00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:28.471
In the attention economy, our time is the product.

7
00:00:29.171 --> 00:00:32.450
Your turn. Say: attention economy.

8
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:36.674
attention economy.

9
00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:39.010
infinite scroll.

10
00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:45.877
infinite scroll means a design where new content loads endlessly as you scroll.

11
00:00:46.177 --> 00:00:49.175
Infinite scroll makes it hard to stop using an app.

12
00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:52.899
Your turn. Say: infinite scroll.

13
00:00:55.599 --> 00:00:56.844
infinite scroll.

14
00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:58.747
dopamine.

15
00:00:59.447 --> 00:01:03.037
dopamine means a brain chemical linked to pleasure and reward.

16
00:01:03.337 --> 00:01:06.544
Each notification gives a small hit of dopamine.

17
00:01:07.244 --> 00:01:09.722
Your turn. Say: dopamine.

18
00:01:12.422 --> 00:01:13.423
dopamine.

19
00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:16.045
engagement metric.

20
00:01:16.745 --> 00:01:22.111
engagement metric means a measurement of how much users interact with content.

21
00:01:22.411 --> 00:01:25.595
Likes and shares are key engagement metrics.

22
00:01:26.295 --> 00:01:29.516
Your turn. Say: engagement metric.

23
00:01:32.216 --> 00:01:33.786
engagement metric.

24
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:36.214
persuasive design.

25
00:01:36.914 --> 00:01:41.816
persuasive design means designing technology to change users' behaviour.

26
00:01:42.116 --> 00:01:46.484
Red notification dots are an example of persuasive design.

27
00:01:47.184 --> 00:01:50.591
Your turn. Say: persuasive design.

28
00:01:53.291 --> 00:01:55.047
persuasive design.

29
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.817
notification fatigue.

30
00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:03.234
notification fatigue means tiredness caused by constant alerts.

31
00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:07.042
Notification fatigue made her turn off every alert.

32
00:02:07.742 --> 00:02:11.416
Your turn. Say: notification fatigue.

33
00:02:14.116 --> 00:02:15.814
notification fatigue.

34
00:02:16.817 --> 00:02:18.212
digital wellbeing.

35
00:02:18.912 --> 00:02:22.607
digital wellbeing means a healthy relationship with technology.

36
00:02:22.907 --> 00:02:26.126
Phones now include digital wellbeing tools.

37
00:02:26.826 --> 00:02:30.430
Your turn. Say: digital wellbeing.

38
00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.305
digital wellbeing.

39
00:02:35.317 --> 00:02:36.283
clickstream.

40
00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:41.003
clickstream means the record of everything a user clicks online.

41
00:02:41.303 --> 00:02:44.870
Advertisers analyse our clickstream to target us.

42
00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.582
Your turn. Say: clickstream.

43
00:02:51.282 --> 00:02:52.144
clickstream.

44
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.393
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:55.693 --> 00:02:59.051
I spent four hours on my phone yesterday without noticing.

46
00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:05.127
That's the attention economy at work. Moreover, it's carefully designed.

47
00:03:06.417 --> 00:03:08.962
You mean infinite scroll and notifications?

48
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:15.317
Exactly. In other words, persuasive design keeps you hooked.

49
00:03:16.617 --> 00:03:20.093
Still, I enjoy social media. It isn't all bad.

50
00:03:21.383 --> 00:03:26.892
Of course. Nevertheless, engagement metrics reward outrage more than truth.

51
00:03:28.183 --> 00:03:29.533
So what should I do?

52
00:03:30.817 --> 00:03:36.267
To begin with, turn off non-essential notifications. Then, set clear time limits.

53
00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:40.245
Grammar focus: Discourse markers.

54
00:03:40.545 --> 00:03:43.891
Discourse markers organise your ideas and guide the listener.

55
00:03:44.191 --> 00:03:49.139
Moreover adds, nevertheless contrasts, and in other words clarifies.

56
00:03:49.439 --> 00:03:56.840
For example: Moreover, the design is deliberate. Nevertheless, social media can be useful.

57
00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:02.193
Discourse markers guide your listener through an argument; avoid overusing the same one.

58
00:04:03.183 --> 00:04:06.567
Now read and listen. Your Attention Is for Sale.

59
00:04:06.867 --> 00:04:09.703
Many of the most popular apps are free to use.

60
00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:15.779
However, as the saying goes, if you are not paying, you are the product.

61
00:04:17.083 --> 00:04:22.926
In the attention economy, companies earn money by keeping us online for as long as possible.

62
00:04:24.217 --> 00:04:30.187
To achieve this, designers use features such as infinite scroll and unpredictable rewards.

63
00:04:31.483 --> 00:04:37.140
Moreover, engagement metrics tend to favour emotional and controversial content.

64
00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:44.269
Each notification triggers a small release of dopamine, which keeps us checking our phones.

65
00:04:45.583 --> 00:04:51.484
Nevertheless, users are not powerless; digital wellbeing tools can help us regain control.

66
00:04:52.783 --> 00:04:57.314
In other words, attention is a resource, and we can choose how to spend it.

67
00:04:58.617 --> 00:05:01.466
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:01.766 --> 00:05:05.066
A brain chemical linked to pleasure and reward is blank.

69
00:05:05.366 --> 00:05:10.529
A, dopamine. B, clickstream. C, engagement metric.

70
00:05:14.517 --> 00:05:16.946
The answer is A: dopamine.

71
00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:21.634
The app is free. blank, it collects a lot of data.

72
00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:25.959
A, Moreover. B, Nevertheless. C, In other words.

73
00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.820
The answer is B: Nevertheless.

74
00:05:34.317 --> 00:05:37.663
The task is hard. blank, it requires patience.

75
00:05:37.963 --> 00:05:42.696
A, In other words. B, Nevertheless. C, Still.

76
00:05:46.683 --> 00:05:48.903
The answer is A: In other words.

77
00:05:50.417 --> 00:05:53.612
Tiredness caused by constant alerts is blank.

78
00:05:53.912 --> 00:06:00.224
A, digital wellbeing. B, notification fatigue. C, persuasive design.

79
00:06:04.217 --> 00:06:07.215
The answer is B: notification fatigue.

80
00:06:08.717 --> 00:06:12.818
According to the text, engagement metrics tend to favour blank.

81
00:06:13.118 --> 00:06:18.977
A, calm content. B, emotional and controversial content. C, long articles.

82
00:06:22.983 --> 00:06:27.862
The answer is B: emotional and controversial content.

83
00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:34.766
Now it's your turn. Part one: Do tech companies have too much control over our attention?

84
00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:46.105
Part two: Use at least three discourse markers to give your view, reasons and a solution.

85
00:06:52.405 --> 00:07:05.985
To begin with, I believe they do. Moreover, their designs exploit our psychology. Nevertheless, users can take back control: in other words, we should turn off notifications and set daily limits.

86
00:07:06.285 --> 00:07:09.109
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:10.117 --> 00:07:14.406
Great work today! You learned eight new words and discourse markers.

88
00:07:14.706 --> 00:07:18.322
Today's value is good habits. Do good things every day.

89
00:07:18.622 --> 00:07:21.367
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:21.667 --> 00:07:24.494
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

